From 7adccb60fd6304876b32aaeee0ed336617c1a0a4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: overdoingism <overdoingism@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 22:37:36 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 17/18] rdna-boosts: block 17: TQ1_0 / TQ2_0 (ternary) weights
 on the GPU -- MUL_MAT and GET_ROWS, bit-identical to the CPU backend's AVX2
 path

Ternary weights (TQ1_0 / TQ2_0) had no GPU path: supports_op refused them, so the weights stayed in host memory and the
CPU backend ran every matmul over them, and a ternary model (BitNet b1.58 and its conversions) decoded at CPU speed with
-ngl 99.  This adds MUL_MAT (not MUL_MAT_ID), GET_ROWS and the to_fp16 / fp32 / bf16 conversions.

- The MUL_MAT result is bit-identical to the CPU backend's x86 AVX2 path (ggml_vec_dot_tq{1,2}_0_q8_K), for every batch
  size: src1 is quantized to Q8_K as quantize_row_q8_K_ref does, each 256-value block's integer dot is split into the
  CPU's eight 32-bit lanes (lane L takes the values whose index mod 32 is 4L..4L+3, minus the Q8_K bsums of values
  32L..32L+31), each lane is accumulated over the blocks with an UNFUSED mul and add by y.d*x.d, and the eight lanes are
  summed in hsum_float_8's order (tq.cuh).  The mul/add is not fused into an fma: a Release ggml-cpu build emits
  `vmulps` + `vaddps` for the CPU's `_mm256_add_ps(_mm256_mul_ps(..), sumf)` and no `vfmadd`, so `fmaf` in the kernels is
  a 1-ULP-per-block difference from the CPU (which shows up as a nonzero test-backend-ops NMSE on every k > 256 -- see
  the note in tq.cu).  Both TQ kernels, `GGML_CUDA_CC_IS_RDNA4`'s int8-WMMA one included, use the same unfused add.
- Decode (one src1 column): a vec kernel that quantizes src1 into shared memory itself (no quantize launch).  With
  GGML_CUDA_TQ_FUSE (on unless 0) it also takes the Q, K and V projections in one launch, gate + up + SWIGLU (written as
  the GLU kernel computes it), and a matmul + its residual ADD; and graph_optimize moves the V / K projections directly
  behind the Q projection so that the three are adjacent, on one stream.  Every fused result is that of the separate ops
  on this backend, bit for bit.
- 2..16 columns: a quantize kernel and a small-batch kernel (dp4a).  Larger batches on RDNA4: int8 WMMA, one K = 32 MMA
  per block and CPU lane, the accumulator starting at minus the lane's Q8_K sum (TQ1_0 is converted to TQ2_0 first -- the
  same values -- and that conversion reads the weight flat, so a strided TQ1_0 weight keeps the small-batch kernel,
  which takes every batch).  Elsewhere the small-batch kernel takes every batch.
- GGML_CUDA_TQ=0 refuses the types again (the previous behaviour, the CPU backend runs them);
  GGML_CUDA_TQ_CENSUS=1 logs the launches per path when the context is freed.

Measured on one R9700 (gfx1201), llama-bench -fa 1 -p 512 -n 128 -r 3, mean t/s, against GGML_CUDA_TQ=0 (the CPU backend,
i.e. the behaviour before this block): Llama3-8B-1.58 TQ2_0 pp512 4177 vs 258, tg128 181.5 vs 21.8; TQ1_0 3693 vs 99.5,
198.2 vs 16.1.  Same-seed greedy text is identical in all three arms (the default, GGML_CUDA_TQ_FUSE=0 and
GGML_CUDA_TQ=0) on both models, on 1 GPU and on a 3-GPU -sm layer / -sm tensor split, and identical over a 200-token
generation; test-backend-ops -o MUL_MAT -p type_a=tq is 61/61 with LLAMA_TEST_OP_NMSE=1 reading exactly 0 on all 305
reported values, and GET_ROWS 16/16 with all 84 reading exactly 0.  A non-TQ model's text and throughput are unchanged
(gfx1201 and gfx1100/gfx1151 arms in the release record).

Not covered by this block: MUL_MAT_ID (a MoE with TQ experts keeps them on the CPU backend), draft/MTP decoding, a
non-x86 CPU reference, and the CUDA (nvcc) build of tq.cu -- only the HIP build is compiled here.

Gate: scripts/gate-tq.sh.  Knobs: ENVIRONMENT.md section 25.  Record: archive/work/tq-hip/.

